 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "KELVINGROVE. Red sandstone, built for the 1901 Exhibition. It has a Spitfire hanging from the ceiling, an elephant called Sir Roger, a Dalí, and an organ that plays most days. Entry is free. Ask for the elephant.",
   "x": 11.0,
   "y": 13.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GEORGE SQUARE. The front lawn of the City Chambers, lined with statues, with Walter Scott up on a very tall column. It has hosted Christmas markets, film crews and one truly ambitious pigeon.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 18.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE DUKE AND HIS CONE. The Duke of Wellington statue nearly always wears a traffic cone. The cone has been removed many times. Glasgow has put it back many times more.",
   "x": 49.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "text": "SQUINTY BRIDGE. Officially the Clyde Arc, opened in 2006. The arch leans to one side and the deck curves, so everyone calls it the Squinty Bridge. Nobody has ever called it the Clyde Arc without a wee smile.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CLYDE. The river that built the city. Once the shipyards on its banks launched a big share of the world's ships. Today its quays are for walking, cycling and watching the water change colour.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE FINNIESTON CRANE. A giant crane from 1931, built to lift locomotives onto ships bound all over the world. It's a monument now, and still looks like it could lift your house.",
   "x": 51.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GLASCHU. Often translated 'the dear green place'. St Mungo's rhyme is on the city's arms: the bird that never flew, the tree that never grew, the bell that never rang and the fish that never swam.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE NECROPOLIS. A Victorian city of the dead on the hill behind the cathedral, with John Knox up on the tallest column. Grand, quiet, and the best free view in Glasgow.",
   "x": 63,
   "y": 9
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "glasgow_lord_kelvin",
   "title": "Lord Kelvin",
   "text": "William Thomson taught at Glasgow University for over fifty years and helped lay the first transatlantic telegraph cable. Made a lord, he took his title from the River Kelvin, which runs past the university. The Kelvin temperature scale is named after him, and by way of the river, so is this gallery."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 51,
   "plaque": "glasgow_first_international",
   "title": "The First International",
   "text": "On 30 November 1872 Scotland played England at a cricket ground in Partick, in the West End, in the first official international football match. It finished 0 to 0, in front of about 4,000 people. Both sides have been arguing about it ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "glasgow_irn_bru",
   "title": "Irn-Bru",
   "text": "A.G. Barr, a soft drinks firm that started in Falkirk and moved to Glasgow, launched a bright orange fizzy drink called Iron Brew in 1901. It later became Irn-Bru, and in Scotland it has long outsold Coca-Cola, which very few countries can say. The recipe is a secret. The colour is not."
  },
  {
   "text": "WEST ROAD. Loch Lomond, the Trossachs and on to Oban, Gateway to the Isles. Take the high road. Or the low road. The low road has fewer cyclists.",
   "x": 3,
   "y": 27
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "glasgow_arandora_star",
   "title": "The Arandora Star",
   "text": "When Italy joined the war in June 1940, Italian men all over Britain, many of them cafe owners who had lived here for decades, were rounded up as enemy aliens. On 2 July the Arandora Star, taking internees to Canada, was sunk by a U-boat off Ireland, and more than 400 Italians died, many from Scotland. A garden at St Andrew's Cathedral in Glasgow remembers them.",
   "people": "italians"
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "glasgow_strathclyde",
   "people": "celts",
   "title": "Kingdom of Strathclyde",
   "text": "For centuries the Clyde valley was a kingdom of Britons, who spoke a Brittonic language close to Old Welsh. Its stronghold was Dumbarton Rock, Alt Clut, 'rock of the Clyde'. St Mungo is said to have been its bishop, and Glasgow's own name is probably Brittonic too."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Charles Rennie Mackintosh, architect and designer, studied in Glasgow and designed the School of Art, begun in 1897.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "glasgow_mackintosh",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Charles Rennie Mackintosh"
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "arts_margaret_macdonald",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Margaret Macdonald Mackintosh",
   "text": "Margaret Macdonald, born in 1864, studied at the Glasgow School of Art, where she, her sister Frances, Herbert MacNair and Charles Rennie Mackintosh were 'the Four', and their tall, pale, flowing figures became the Glasgow Style. She married Mackintosh in 1900 and made the gesso panels for Miss Cranston's Willow Tea Rooms on Sauchiehall Street in 1903. Mackintosh is said to have put it this way: Margaret had genius, he had only talent. She died in 1933. Look for the roses; they are usually hers."
  }
 ],
